  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Signal Energy",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert instantaneous signal power into signal energy by integrating $|x(t)|^2$ over all time and interpreting finite or infinite totals as the signal-size basis for later energy, decaying-signal, and power classifications.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signal Energy",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Signal Energy",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Signal energy is the total accumulated size of a signal, not its average size. When the whole waveform matters, start by recognizing the instantaneous power built from $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t)=|x(t)|^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Once you have $p(t)$, compute signal energy by integrating that power over all time to get one total number, $E$, for the signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This same definition classifies signals: if the integral is finite, the signal has finite energy, and if the integral is infinite, it does not. The finite-versus-infinite total is the signal-size test used later in the module.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_0^T A^2\\,dt=A^2T",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a constant pulse of amplitude $A$ on a finite interval from $0$ to $T$, the definition collapses to a concrete total: the energy is proportional to the pulse height squared and its duration. That is the simplest base case for the idea.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signal Energy",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Converting Instantaneous Power into an Energy Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=3$ for $0\\le t<2$ and $0$ otherwise, write the total energy as an integral of $|x(t)|^2$ and simplify the integrand.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a finite-duration pulse: it is constant on its support and zero elsewhere. For energy, keep the nonzero interval and use the squared magnitude as the integrand.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^2 |3|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the signal equals $3$ on that interval, the instantaneous power is constant and equals $|3|^2=9$. The limits stay at the nonzero interval because the signal is zero everywhere else.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_0^2 9\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the correct energy setup: square the magnitude, restrict the integral to the support, and leave the definite integral unevaluated for now.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signal Energy",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating a Finite Energy Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the total energy of $x(t)=2$ for $0\\le t<3$ and $0$ otherwise.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a finite-duration constant pulse, so use the all-time energy definition $E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt$ and restrict it to the nonzero interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^3 |2|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the amplitude is constant, the integrand is the constant power level $|2|^2=4$ on $0\\le t<3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_0^3 4\\,dt=12",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The total energy is $12$. This is the finished scalar value for the signal's size.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signal Energy",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Identifying Infinite Signal Energy",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine whether $x(t)=\\cos(2\\pi t)$ has finite or infinite energy, and justify the answer from the energy integral.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a persistent periodic signal, so start from the all-time energy definition $E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt$ and check whether the integrand keeps contributing for every time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\cos^2(2\\pi t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "$\\cos^2(2\\pi t)$ is nonnegative and repeats forever, so the signal never stops adding positive area to the energy integral. Bounded amplitude only keeps $|x(t)|$ small; it does not make the total energy finite.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So this signal has infinite energy.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signal Energy",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Comparing Signals by Total Energy",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare the total energies of $x_1(t)=2$ for $0\\le t<2$ and $x_2(t)=1$ for $0\\le t<5$, and state which is larger.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a side-by-side comparison, so compute each signal's total energy with the same definition and compare the resulting scalars.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E_1=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x_1(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^2 |2|^2\\,dt=8",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $x_2(t)$, the amplitude is smaller but the signal lasts longer, so square the amplitude and integrate over its full support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E_2=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x_2(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^5 |1|^2\\,dt=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $E_1=8$ and $E_2=5$, $x_1(t)$ has the larger total energy. The result shows that total energy depends on both amplitude squared and duration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
